Subject: [PATCH 023/154] atrium: P0 - chrome://atrium.top-chrome host and
 reserved context
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

First step of the SwiftUI-to-WebUI migration (decision-1). Registers the host,
the resource pipeline, the reserved BrowserContext every chrome surface will
render in, and crash recovery for the shared renderer. Renders nothing real yet.

## The host

AtriumUI derives from content::WebUIController, not TopChromeWebUIController.
The latter would work - passing enable_chrome_send=true does grant chrome.send,
as OmniboxPopupUI does - but it derives from ui::MojoWebUIController, which sets
kMojoWebUi unconditionally (ui/webui/mojo_web_ui_controller.cc:14-16). The
transport decision is chrome.send, so Mojo bindings would be unused privilege on
a page that already holds browser-level reach. What the base class contributes
is the zoom pin and the display disposition; both are replicated.

Resources are a hand-written .grd: build_webui asserts static_js_files == [] and
so cannot carry plain JS. The data source is CreateAndAdd + AddResourcePaths +
SetDefaultResource and nothing else - deliberately NOT
webui::SetupWebUIDataSource, which calls OverrideContentSecurityPolicy(ScriptSrc)
and installs a non-empty trusted-types allowlist. Both would undo the chosen
option A. The raw defaults leave trusted-types empty, so createPolicy() throws.

The .top-chrome suffix is functional: it is what makes
ChromeRenderWidgetHostViewMacDelegate return kWhenInActiveApp, so clicks land on
an inactive window without a focusing first click. Zero Atrium lines needed.

## The reserved context

Every chrome WebContents renders in the system profile's OTR context. Atrium
binds one BrowserController per profile per slot, so per-presenter contexts
would mean one chrome renderer per profile; one reserved context collapses the
layer to a single renderer regardless of window or profile count.

CreateChromeContents always passes the shared SiteInstance explicitly, which
bypasses the IsSuitableHost policy walk and the random-existing-host choice - so
no process-limit or site-isolation heuristic can split the chrome layer.

The keep-alive goes on GetOriginalProfile(): ScopedProfileKeepAlive does not
support OTR profiles. TryAcquire rather than the constructor, because
ProfileManager refuses one on a profile already at refcount zero; if it returns
null the context stays unset and the UI stays disabled rather than racing
teardown.

IsWebUIEnabled compares against that context, and null (before async creation
completes) never equals a real context - so the UI is disabled until ready,
which is the correct fail-closed direction and why it must not CHECK.

## Crash recovery

One renderer for the whole chrome layer means one crash takes down the sidebar,
command bar, peek, pill and settings together, so recovery is not optional the
way it is for a dismissible bubble. AtriumChromeContentsObserver reloads the
route. Deliberately unlike upstream, whose WebUIContentsWrapper closes the UI
(webui_contents_wrapper.cc:285-288) - correct for a bubble the user can reopen,
wrong for window furniture. Capped at 3 reloads/minute: a renderer that crashes
on load would otherwise loop forever and bury the original crash.

## Upstream test-list entries

Registering any WebUIConfig breaks three upstream browsertests, which enumerate
every registered config regardless of IsWebUIEnabled. Entries added to
webui_urls_for_test.cc (kChromeUntestedUrls, same posture as
webui-toolbar.top-chrome), enums.xml (WebUIUrlHashes) and histograms.xml
(WebUIHost). The hash value was read out of the test's own failure message
rather than computed.

## Verified

Build clean. Zero resource-ID drift - the diff against default_resource_ids is
one added block, nothing realigned. The resource map carries SOURCE paths
("../../chrome/browser/resources/atrium/atrium.html"), not gen/ paths, so the
load_webui_from_disk reload loop works for this grd. Host registered and
rendered disabled from a user profile, confirmed over CDP. Reserved context
creation confirmed by log, with a control line proving INFO was visible.

browser_tests: all five green - the three upstream tests above plus
AtriumUIBrowserTest.UserProfileCannotReachTheChromeUI and
ContextAbsentWithoutTheDylib.

The first version of UserProfileCannotReachTheChromeUI asserted the wrong
things: that the navigation must fail, and that no top-chrome process exists.
Both are proxies, and both are wrong - an error page legitimately commits at the
requested URL, and RenderProcessFlags::kForTopChromeWebUI is set from the
lexical host, not from whether a controller was created. It now asserts the
actual property: GetWebUI() is null and the frame carries no bindings.

Not yet proven: that the UI is ENABLED inside the reserved context. That needs a
WebContents created there, which arrives with the first real surface (P1).

